Kind Declared First value Local variable inside a method, constructor or block none: you must assign it before reading it Instance variable (field) in the class, outside methods automatic default, when the object is created Class variable (<code>static</code> field) in the class, with <code>static</code> automatic default, when the class is set up <pre><code>public class Movie {
    static int totalMovies;   // class variable: 0
    int views;                // instance variable: 0

    public void play() {
        int count;            // local variable: no value
        System.out.println(count);   // ❌ doesn't compile
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Java never guesses a value for a local variable. Reading one before assigning it is a compiler error.</p>

Case What it is Who decides which method runs Overload Same name, different parameters The compiler, from the argument types Override Instance method, same signature as an inherited one The real object, at runtime Hide Static method, same signature as an inherited one The reference type Redeclare Same signature as a <code>private</code> parent method Nothing to choose: it is a new method <p>To override, four rules must hold: the same signature, the same or wider access, no new or broader checked exception, and the same return type or a subtype (covariant return). A <code>final</code> method can never be overridden or hidden.</p> <pre><code>import java.io.IOException;

class Player {
    protected Number play() throws IOException { return 1; }
}

class TvPlayer extends Player {
    @Override
    public Integer play() { return 2; }   // wider access, no exception, covariant return
}
</code></pre> <p>→ Rewriting What Your Parent Class Does</p>

Side Members Can call instance abstract, <code>default</code>, <code>private</code> every member of the interface class <code>static</code>, <code>private static</code> class members only <pre><code>interface Catalog {
    String name();
    default String show() { return name() + count(); }   // OK: there is an object
    private static int count() { return 3; }
}
</code></pre> <p>Adding <code>static String top() { return show(); }</code> to <code>Catalog</code> doesn't compile: a static method has no object to call <code>show()</code> on.</p> <p>The lab puts every kind of method into one small program.</p> <p>💻 Code: Java17_Part_7_Interfaces_Abstract_Types on GitHub</p> <p>← Implicit modifiers and duplicate methods · Next: the lab →</p>

Interface Method In → out <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code> <code>get()</code> nothing → <code>T</code> <code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>BiConsumer&lt;T,U&gt;</code> <code>accept</code> 1 or 2 → nothing <code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>BiPredicate&lt;T,U&gt;</code> <code>test</code> 1 or 2 → <code>boolean</code> <code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code> / <code>BiFunction&lt;T,U,R&gt;</code> <code>apply</code> 1 or 2 → <code>R</code> <code>UnaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>BinaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code> <code>apply</code> <code>T</code> (or <code>T, T</code>) → <code>T</code> <p>Count what goes in, check what comes out. Primitive versions (<code>IntSupplier</code>, <code>DoublePredicate</code>, <code>ToIntFunction&lt;T&gt;</code>…) avoid boxing and rename their methods: <code>getAsBoolean()</code>, <code>applyAsInt()</code>…</p> <p>They combine without rewriting logic: <code>p.and(q)</code>, <code>p.or(q)</code>, <code>p.negate()</code>; <code>f.andThen(g)</code> runs <code>f</code> first, <code>f.compose(g)</code> runs <code>g</code> first:</p> <pre><code>Function&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; addAd = s -&gt; s + 30;
Function&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; twice = s -&gt; s * 2;
twice.compose(addAd).apply(60);   // (60 + 30) * 2 = 180
twice.andThen(addAd).apply(60);   // 60 * 2 + 30 = 150
</code></pre> <p>→ The Ready-Made Lambda Toolkit</p>
